Choose Between a Ruck Plate and Plate Carrier

Carry a plate in a backpack if you are new to rucking or want to go long: it holds water and layers and gives you the most room to adjust the fit. Add GORUCK's Ruck Plate Carrier for shorter plate-only sessions, or a front-and-back vest if you want training that resembles body armor. Whichever you choose, start lighter than your backpack load when you switch.

Frequently Asked Questions

A backpack with a plate pocket covers more for a beginner, because it also carries water and layers. GORUCK's Ruck Plate Carrier 3.0 (Standard) suits plate-only sessions: it holds one 10, 20 or 30 lb Standard plate high on the back and has no other storage. In either setup, start with 5 to 10 lb for 20 to 30 minutes on level ground.

GORUCK Ruck Plates have flat surfaces and rounded corners made for compatible plate pockets. That purpose-built fit may justify the higher price for frequent use, but a lower-cost plate can also work if its dimensions match the pocket and the weight can be secured.

Round gym plates are awkward to secure in a pack and can press into your back if they shift. A flat plate with rounded edges is easier to hold in place, but only if its dimensions match the pocket. GORUCK's Standard carrier pocket is 9.75 × 11.75 in, so a generic 10 × 12 in plate will not fit it.

Start with 5 to 10 lb for 20 to 30 minutes on level ground, whatever your fitness level. GORUCK's lightest plate is the 10 lb Standard. Add time before weight, and increase the load only once the current one feels routine.

